    <node id="LOBOS MARINOS DE DOS PELOS">
      <data key="d0">SPECIES</data>
      <data key="d1"> Los lobos marinos de dos pelos (Arctocephalus australis) are a species of pinnipeds, also known as marine mammals, found in the waters surrounding South America. They are specifically known to inhabit the coasts of Argentina, with a population of approximately 1850 individuals. These animals are grouped into two main colonies, one in Isla Escondida to the north and the other in Isla de los Estados to the south. They are recognized for their social behavior and colony lifestyle.

These particular species of pinnipeds are unique for their distinctive coat, which has two types of hair. They can be found in various locations along the southern coasts, including Bah&#237;a Flinders, Bah&#237;a Crossley, and other sites. Despite being called "lobos marinos de dos pelos" in Spanish, or "two-haired sea lions" in English, they are not actually sea lions but are more closely related to seals.

In summary, the lobos marinos de dos pelos are fascinating marine mammals with a distinct coat and social behavior, inhabiting the southern coasts of South America, particularly in Argentina, with a population of 1850 individuals divided into two main colonies.</data>
      <data key="d2">0b772cca872bd1596e837c48e534ed7a,2d041f866c2a7f149216961d43696c82,34798580ffa1eeacedf7b69c5f9ef85d,882fdf01528bf45b0f6d55ba9924f551,8a0c5d2be39b9a9e02f97cbc7551030c,9d8e3504b4ee32dcb2cfe65980dddcaa,acce0f2d52d35b868047d606555fc1e4,cf58f0416a8d27360f6d6e772471b831</data>
      <data key="d6">59</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">20</data>
      <data key="d4">257</data>
      <data key="d5">88c723ff37764ceabec761d8e1ce4ff7</data>
    </node>
