    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A LAS PIEDRAS">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1"> "Lober&#237;a Las Piedras is a permanent habitat colony of sea lions located in the Bah&#237;a Blanca - Cabo V&#237;rgenes region. In the year 1950, it was home to 135 individuals. This habitat is situated to the east of Caleta Falso Policarpo and approximately 700 meters from the previous rookery. The regional topography includes rocky beaches, large and medium-sized stones, partly smooth pebbles, and sand, with nearby cliffs that are approximately 20 meters high and covered in typical vegetation."</data>
      <data key="d2">55cd91801b46fb522e3565c9e40ffb77,a48ce0dd8016f44c624e3bc01162c4c5</data>
      <data key="d6">63</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">439</data>
      <data key="d5">70bf07288497425da9abe79bdc3d562f</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A LAS CHAPAS">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1"> "Lober&#237;a Las Chapas is a permanent habitat zone for sea lions, located in the Bah&#237;a Blanca - Cabo V&#237;rgenes region, specifically to the southeast of the Las Piedras colony and adjacent to the mouth of a deep canyon. It is approximately 400 meters from the Las Piedras colony. The local topography includes cliffs, a beach with some pebbles and sand at the mouth of a nearby stream, and steep coastlines of around 300 meters in height. The land is marshy with typical vegetation and low shrubs. In the year 1950, the colony had 136 sea lions."</data>
      <data key="d2">55cd91801b46fb522e3565c9e40ffb77,a48ce0dd8016f44c624e3bc01162c4c5</data>
      <data key="d6">63</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">440</data>
      <data key="d5">7d021885a4664a13bef26a296476343e</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A TRES AMIGOS">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1"> "LOBER&#205;A TRES AMIGOS" is a permanent habitat colony of sea lions located in the Bah&#237;a Blanca - Cabo V&#237;rgenes region. In the year 1950, the colony was home to 137 individuals. The topography of the area is characterized by rocky beaches that extend into a long reef or restinga, with some areas of pebble stone and cliffs reaching approximately 30 meters high. The location of this colony is to the east of a previously mentioned sea lion habitat, and it is situated about 450 meters from Cabo San Diego.</data>
      <data key="d2">55cd91801b46fb522e3565c9e40ffb77,a48ce0dd8016f44c624e3bc01162c4c5</data>
      <data key="d6">63</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">2</data>
      <data key="d4">441</data>
      <data key="d5">9f69c02f1e0140de815abbb694d32068</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A CABO SAN VICENTE">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1"> "Lober&#237;a Cabo San Vicente is a permanent habitat for sea lions, specifically located in the N.W. region of Bah&#237;a Thetis, known locally as the lober&#237;a &#8220;Del Cabo&#8221;. This colony of sea lions was once home to 138 individuals in the year 1950. The topography of the area includes cliffs, rocky beaches, some pebbled areas, a long stretching reef at the tip, and a coast that drops off sharply to around 30 meters in height, covered in vegetation predominantly featuring low shrubs, common to the zone."</data>
      <data key="d2">55cd91801b46fb522e3565c9e40ffb77,a48ce0dd8016f44c624e3bc01162c4c5</data>
      <data key="d6">9</data>
