      <data key="d5">20e6042b4aa04caba9755978443ed6bf</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAH&#205;A VALENT&#205;N">
      <data key="d0">NONE</data>
      <data key="d1"> "BAH&#205;A VALENT&#205;N" is a bay located on the west side of Cabo Buen Suceso, between it and Cabo Hall, along the southern coast of Tierra del Fuego. It is known for its biodiversity and its importance in the conservation of pinnipeds, marine mammals such as seals and sea lions. Additionally, there is a second "Bah&#237;a Valent&#237;n" located on the east coast of Isla de los Estados. This bay is also recognized for its high levels of biodiversity and its significance in the preservation of pinnipeds.</data>
      <data key="d2">8a0c5d2be39b9a9e02f97cbc7551030c,a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">58</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">468</data>
      <data key="d5">e6d23668fa124158a3f73e0007163168</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A &#8220;CABO HALL&#8221;">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1">Lober&#237;a Cabo Hall es una zona de cr&#237;a y parto de lobos marinos, ubicada en la costa desde los extremos Este y Oeste de Cabo Hall, entrada oriental de Bah&#237;a Aguirre.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">111</data>
      <data key="d7">2</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">469</data>
      <data key="d5">82fa3bb110374a1a98dd4b98510e5db7</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CABO HALL">
      <data key="d0">NONE</data>
      <data key="d1"> Cabo Hall is a cape located in the entrance oriental of Bah&#237;a Aguirre, on the south coast of Tierra del Fuego. Alternatively, there is a cape also named Cabo Hall, which is situated on the west coast of Isla de los Estados. This second location is known for its high biodiversity and its importance in the conservation of pinnipeds. Given the two descriptions, it is possible that there are two different geographical features sharing the same name, each with its own distinct characteristics and environmental significance.</data>
      <data key="d2">8a0c5d2be39b9a9e02f97cbc7551030c,a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">58</data>
      <data key="d7">1</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">470</data>
      <data key="d5">bef1481d3cd745d0a8fa3ce2df3a9f4c</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A &#8220;SAN MART&#205;N DE TOURS&#8221;">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1">Lober&#237;a San Mart&#237;n de Tours es una zona de cr&#237;a y parto de lobos marinos, ubicada en un islote en el centro de la Bah&#237;a Sloggett.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">111</data>
      <data key="d7">2</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">471</data>
      <data key="d5">b8eca5a6ccb7466683c94f9907e88eb4</data>
    </node>
    <node id="BAH&#205;A SLOGGETT">
      <data key="d0">NONE</data>
      <data key="d1">Bah&#237;a Sloggett es una bah&#237;a ubicada en la costa sur de Tierra del Fuego.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">472</data>
      <data key="d5">e1569262a69b49119d509af73a82b78d</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISLAS BECASSES">
      <data key="d0">NONE</data>
      <data key="d1">Islas Becasses son un grupo de islas ubicadas en el centro del canal Moat, en la costa sur de Tierra del Fuego.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">473</data>
      <data key="d5">71bdebfef2964fb7a611d61cef063266</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A &#8220;ISLA BECASSES&#8221;">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1">Lober&#237;a Isla Becasses es una zona de cr&#237;a y parto de lobos marinos, ubicada en la isla m&#225;s grande y occidental del grupo de islas Becasses.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">111</data>
      <data key="d7">2</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">474</data>
      <data key="d5">b95e214349194209b679488a9741f978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ISLA YUNQUE">
      <data key="d0">NONE</data>
      <data key="d1"> Isla Yunque is a southern Argentine island located in the Canal de Beagle, specifically to the east of Isla Gable and southwest of Bah&#237;a Thouctof. This island is recognized for its high biodiversity and its significance in the conservation of pinnipeds, which are a group of fin-footed mammals that include seals, sea lions, and walruses.</data>
      <data key="d2">8a0c5d2be39b9a9e02f97cbc7551030c,a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d3">0</data>
      <data key="d4">475</data>
      <data key="d5">fd4cfb19f38a484c9dab688753258e30</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LOBER&#205;A &#8220;ISLA YUNQUE&#8221;">
      <data key="d0">HABITAT</data>
      <data key="d1">Lober&#237;a Isla Yunque es una zona de cr&#237;a y parto de lobos marinos, ubicada en la isla Yunque en el Canal de Beagle.</data>
      <data key="d2">a4db5e0e4291abc5a30297f97a8ac533</data>
      <data key="d6">111</data>
      <data key="d7">2</data>
      <data key="d3">1</data>
      <data key="d4">476</data>
      <data key="d5">0e2164a2ed8349758880f63e28d2ccdb</data>
    </node>
